 1. format("%.2f", 3.14159)                    -> "3.14"                     fixed decimals; "%.2f" % 3.14159 is the String#% spelling
 2. format("%08.3f", 3.14159)                  -> "0003.142"                 width 8, zero-filled, 3 decimals
 3. format("%e", 123456.789)                   -> "1.234568e+05"             scientific; %.3e of 0.000123456 is "1.235e-04", %g picks a form: "1.23457e+06"
 4. format("%x %b %o", 255, 255, 255)          -> "ff 11111111 377"          bases without a prefix; %#x %#b %#o give "0xff 0b11111111 0377"
 5. format("%+d|%05d|%-5d|", 5, 42, 42)        -> "+5|00042|42   |"          a sign, zero fill, left alignment
 6. format("%08b", -5)                         -> "..111011"                 a negative in a base is two's complement behind a .. prefix; %x of -255 is "..f01"
 7. format("%.2f", 2.675)                      -> "2.68"                     not 2.67: 2.665 gives 2.66 and 2.685 gives 2.68, half to even on the decimal digits
 8. 1234567.to_s.gsub(/\B(?=(\d{3})+\z)/, ",") -> "1,234,567"                no thousands flag in format, so an idiom; %,d is ArgumentError: malformed format string - %,
 9. 255.to_s(16), 255.to_s(2), 255.to_s(36)    -> "ff", "11111111", "73"     to_s takes a base from 2 to 36
10. "ff".hex, "ff".to_i(16), "0x1f".to_i(0)    -> 255, 255, 31               back from a base; base 0 reads the prefix; "zz".hex is 0, because to_i never fails
11. Integer("0x1f", 16), Integer("0b101"), Integer("1_000") -> 31, 5, 1000                Integer() reads prefixes and underscores, and raises on junk
12. Integer("08")                              -> ArgumentError: invalid value for Integer(): "08" a leading 0 means octal, where 8 is not a digit; "08".to_i is 8
13. (0.1 + 0.2).to_s, 1e20.to_s                -> "0.30000000000000004", "1.0e+20" to_s is the shortest string that reads back as the same Float; 100.0.to_s is "100.0", never without the .0
14. 999999999999999.9.to_s, 1e15.to_s          -> "999999999999999.9", "1.0e+15" exponent form once the integer part has 16 digits; and below 0.0001: 1e-4.to_s is "0.0001", 1e-5.to_s is "1.0e-05"
15. 1234.5678.round(2), .floor(2), .round(-2)  -> 1234.57, 1234.56, 1200     round(-2) returns an Integer; 1250.round(-2) is 1300, half away from zero
16. 3.1.round(2).to_s, format("%.2f", 3.1)     -> "3.1", "3.10"              round drops trailing zeros, format keeps them: money wants format
